r/src/components/common/useUpdateDownloadText.ts#useUpdateDownloadText","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Progress evidence","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":21},{"target":"src/main/installer.ts#runHermesUpdate","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Agent phases","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":51},{"target":"src/main/ipc/register.ts#registerIpcHandlers","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Agent phases","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":51},{"target":"src/renderer/src/components/settings/useSettingsData.ts#useSettingsData","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Agent phases","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":55},{"target":"src/renderer/src/screens/Install/Install.tsx#Install","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Agent phases","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":55},{"target":"tests/release-artifacts.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":127},{"target":"src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":129},{"target":"tests/audit-production-dependencies.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Release security and quality gates","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":141},{"target":"tests/release-artifacts.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Release security and quality gates","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":141},{"target":"tests/audit-production-dependencies.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Release security and quality gates#Audit findings take precedence","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":155},{"target":"tests/macos-signing.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#macOS signing keychain","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":161},{"target":"tests/release-artifacts.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#macOS signing keychain","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":161},{"target":"tests/macos-signing.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#macOS signing keychain#Identity output consumption","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":167},{"target":"tests/release-artifacts.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Native module packaging","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":173},{"target":"tests/packaging-identity.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Production package contents","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":179},{"target":"tests/packaging-identity.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Platform package identity","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":187},{"target":"tests/linux-post-install.test.ts","fromSection":"lat.md/desktop-updates#Desktop Updates#Stable and beta release channels#Linux package sandbox and AppArmor","file":"lat.md/desktop-updates","line":195}],"paragraphs":[{"markdown":"Desktop updates use GitHub releases and expose both a startup upgrade action and a Settings auto-upgrade preference.","startLine":3,"endLine":3,"text":"Desktop updates use GitHub releases and expose both a startup upgrade action and a Settings auto-upgrade preference."},{"markdown":"The Electron main process configures `electron-updater` against the repository publisher metadata from `electron-builder.yml`, which points at `fathah/hermes-desktop`. [[src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater]] registers update IPC handlers, persists the auto-upgrade preference under Electron `userData`, and applies that preference to `autoUpdater.autoDownload`.","startLine":5,"endLine":5,"text":"The Electron main process configures electron-updater against the repository publisher metadata from electron-builder.yml, which points at fathah/hermes-desktop. src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater registers update IPC handlers, persists the auto-upgrade preference under Electron userData, and applies that preference to autoUpdater.autoDownload."},{"markdown":"When GitHub reports a newer release, [[src/renderer/src/screens/Layout/Layout.tsx#Layout]] shows an upgrade button in the sidebar footer as soon as the app reaches the main layout. The button downloads the update when needed, shows download progress, and changes into a restart action after the update is ready.","startLine":7,"endLine":7,"text":"When GitHub reports a newer release, src/renderer/src/screens/Layout/Layout.tsx#Layout shows an upgrade button in the sidebar footer as soon as the app reaches the main layout. The button downloads the update when needed, shows download progress, and changes into a restart action after the update is ready."},{"markdown":"[[src/renderer/src/components/settings/AboutPane.tsx#AboutPane]] (the About & Updates pane of the settings modal) presents the desktop app as its own card, separate from the Hermes Agent engine card — the two update on independent channels. The card shows the app version, the auto-upgrade toggle, and an explicit update action: [[src/renderer/src/components/settings/useSettingsData.ts#useSettingsData]] subscribes to the same `onUpdateAvailable`/`onUpdateDownloadProgress`/`onUpdateDownloaded`/`onUpdateError` events as the footer button and adds a manual `checkDesktopUpdate` (via `checkForUpdates`) plus a `handleDesktopUpdate` that downloads, then restarts via `installUpdate`. When auto-upgrade is enabled the startup release check downloads automatically; when disabled, downloading waits for the user's click (footer button or this card's action).","startLine":9,"endLine":9,"text":"src/renderer/src/components/settings/AboutPane.tsx#AboutPane (the About & Updates pane of the settings modal) presents the desktop app as its own card, separate from the Hermes Agent engine card — the two update on independent channels. The card shows the app version, the auto-upgrade toggle, and an explicit update action: src/renderer/src/components/settings/useSettingsData.ts#useSettingsData subscribes to the same onUpdateAvailable/onUpdateDownloadProgress/onUpdateDownloaded/onUpdateError events as the footer button and adds a manual checkDesktopUpdate (via checkForUpdates) plus a handleDesktopUpdate that downloads, then restarts via installUpdate. When auto-upgrade is enabled the startup release check downloads automatically; when disabled, downloading waits for the user's click (footer button or this card's action)."},{"markdown":"Desktop users install release downloads. npm commands apply to source dependencies and development, rather than a supported desktop app package installed from the registry.","startLine":13,"endLine":13,"text":"Desktop users install release downloads. npm commands apply to source dependencies and development, rather than a supported desktop app package installed from the registry."},{"markdown":"[README](../README.md) directs users to the project download site or GitHub Releases and distinguishes them from the source-checkout commands in the [Contributing Guide](../CONTRIBUTING.md). The README promises the guide's existing dependency and development commands (`npm install`, `npm run dev`), without implying the guide also documents source builds. The package name in `package.json` is build metadata, not an app installation command. Release workflows and `electron-builder.yml` remain the distribution source of truth.","startLine":15,"endLine":15,"text":"README directs users to the project download site or GitHub Releases and distinguishes them from the source-checkout commands in the Contributing Guide. The README promises the guide's existing dependency and development commands (npm install, npm run dev), without implying the guide also documents source builds. The package name in package.json is build metadata, not an app installation command. Release workflows and electron-builder.yml remain the distribution source of truth."},{"markdown":"Desktop downloads preserve measured bytes, speed and fractional percent. Unknown totals and inactive progress remain explicit rather than appearing as a frozen zero-percent download.","startLine":19,"endLine":19,"text":"Desktop downloads preserve measured bytes, speed and fractional percent. Unknown totals and inactive progress remain explicit rather than appearing as a frozen zero-percent download."},{"markdown":"[[src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater]] forwards finite nonnegative metrics through [[src/shared/update-progress.ts#measuredDownloadProgress]]. Percent requires a known positive total; unknown totals omit it. About and the sidebar share [[src/renderer/src/components/common/useUpdateDownloadText.ts#useUpdateDownloadText]], with localized percent and byte formatting. Only `update-downloaded` enables the restart action.","startLine":21,"endLine":21,"text":"src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater forwards finite nonnegative metrics through src/shared/update-progress.ts#measuredDownloadProgress. Percent requires a known positive total; unknown totals omit it. About and the sidebar share src/renderer/src/components/common/useUpdateDownloadText.ts#useUpdateDownloadText, with localized percent and byte formatting. Only update-downloaded enables the restart action."},{"markdown":"A one-shot 15-second deadline is active only while downloading and resets when byte/percent/total measurements change. If no new measurement arrives, the display retains known bytes, hides stale speed and explicitly waits for progress. Completion, error and unmount clear the deadline; there is no idle polling.","startLine":23,"endLine":23,"text":"A one-shot 15-second deadline is active only while downloading and resets when byte/percent/total measurements change. If no new measurement arrives, the display retains known bytes, hides stale speed and explicitly waits for progress. Completion, error and unmount clear the deadline; there is no idle polling."},{"markdown":"This display does not prove differential downloads are used or reduce artifact size. Existing updater packaging and dependency strategies require separate release/runtime evidence before efficiency claims can be resolved.","startLine":25,"endLine":25,"text":"This display does not prove differential downloads are used or reduce artifact size. Existing updater packaging and dependency strategies require separate release/runtime evidence before efficiency claims can be resolved."},{"markdown":"The actual updater registration receives synthetic download events and forwards fractional percent, bytes and speed without executing downloads or installations.","startLine":29,"endLine":29,"text":"The actual updater registration receives synthetic download events and forwards fractional percent, bytes and speed without executing downloads or installations."},{"markdown":"Invalid or unknown totals cannot produce measured percentages. Finite transferred bytes remain useful even when total size and speed are unavailable.","startLine":33,"endLine":33,"text":"Invalid or unknown totals cannot produce measured percentages. Finite transferred bytes remain useful even when total size and speed are unavailable."},{"markdown":"A percentage outside the measured zero-to-one-hundred range remains unknown instead of being clamped into an apparently completed download.","startLine":37,"endLine":37,"text":"A percentage outside the measured zero-to-one-hundred range remains unknown instead of being clamped into an apparently completed download."},{"markdown":"Actual About and Settings state retain byte totals and speed, show fractional progress and wait for the downloaded event before offering restart.","startLine":41,"endLine":41,"text":"Actual About and Settings state retain byte totals and speed, show fractional progress and wait for the downloaded event before offering restart."},{"markdown":"Unknown downloads omit fake zero-percent values. An inactive measurement triggers one waiting deadline, hides stale speed, retains bytes and recovers after progress resumes without persistent polling.","startLine":45,"endLine":45,"text":"Unknown downloads omit fake zero-percent values. An inactive measurement triggers one waiting deadline, hides stale speed, retains bytes and recovers after progress resumes without persistent polling."},{"markdown":"Agent install and update steps describe subprocess phases, not measured work percentages. Running work stays indeterminate; successful IPC completion alone presents a completed result.","startLine":49,"endLine":49,"text":"Agent install and update steps describe subprocess phases, not measured work percentages. Running work stays indeterminate; successful IPC completion alone presents a completed result."},{"markdown":"[[src/main/installer.ts#runHermesUpdate]] emits update-scoped output with unknown step totals while its command runs. [[src/main/ipc/register.ts#registerIpcHandlers]] announces real SSH update, compatibility, gateway, tunnel and connection-refresh operations before awaiting them. Local updates announce compatibility validation after the command exits.","startLine":51,"endLine":51,"text":"src/main/installer.ts#runHermesUpdate emits update-scoped output with unknown step totals while its command runs. src/main/ipc/register.ts#registerIpcHandlers announces real SSH update, compatibility, gateway, tunnel and connection-refresh operations before awaiting them. Local updates announce compatibility validation after the command exits."},{"markdown":"Progress delivery is best effort. A closed or disappearing window cannot interrupt compatibility validation, gateway startup, tunnel reconnection or credential refresh. Delivery failures are isolated from real operation errors, which still fail the update result.","startLine":53,"endLine":53,"text":"Progress delivery is best effort. A closed or disappearing window cannot interrupt compatibility validation, gateway startup, tunnel reconnection or credential refresh. Delivery failures are isolated from real operation errors, which still fail the update result."},{"markdown":"[[src/renderer/src/components/settings/useSettingsData.ts#useSettingsData]] owns an update-only progress subscription correlated with a fresh opaque request ID, rejects late profile or connection reads and releases its listener after completion or unmount. About shows the captured phase and detail. [[src/renderer/src/screens/Install/Install.tsx#Install]] keeps phase labels but presents no running percentage; only its successful result reaches 100 percent.","startLine":55,"endLine":55,"text":"src/renderer/src/components/settings/useSettingsData.ts#useSettingsData owns an update-only progress subscription correlated with a fresh opaque request ID, rejects late profile or connection reads and releases its listener after completion or unmount. About shows the captured phase and detail. src/renderer/src/screens/Install/Install.tsx#Install keeps phase labels but presents no running percentage; only its successful result reaches 100 percent."},{"markdown":"Phase visibility does not claim that upstream dependency work is incremental or optimized. Installer output remains diagnostic evidence, and the original efficiency reports need separate verification.","startLine":57,"endLine":57,"text":"Phase visibility does not claim that upstream dependency work is incremental or optimized. Installer output remains diagnostic evidence, and the original efficiency reports need separate verification."},{"markdown":"About displays only the currently admitted Agent update phase, ignores migration events and clears its listener and phase after the result settles.","startLine":61,"endLine":61,"text":"About displays only the currently admitted Agent update phase, ignores migration events and clears its listener and phase after the result settles."},{"markdown":"Changing the About profile invalidates deferred update phases and results, so old success or failure cannot appear under the new profile.","startLine":65,"endLine":65,"text":"Changing the About profile invalidates deferred update phases and results, so old success or failure cannot appear under the new profile."},{"markdown":"A same-ID connection reload clears the old phase and refuses its delayed callbacks and failure result without borrowing another target's progress.","startLine":69,"endLine":69,"text":"A same-ID connection reload clears the old phase and refuses its delayed callbacks and failure result without borrowing another target's progress."},{"markdown":"Closing About releases an active update listener immediately. A later subprocess result cannot register or release that listener a second time.","startLine":73,"endLine":73,"text":"Closing About releases an active update listener immediately. A later subprocess result cannot register or release that listener a second time."},{"markdown":"Reopening About settles its initial React/config commit before admitting a new update with a distinct real request ID. Old phases and results cannot replace the new job or release its listener.","startLine":77,"endLine":77,"text":"Reopening About settles its initial React/config commit before admitting a new update with a distinct real request ID. Old phases and results cannot replace the new job or release its listener."},{"markdown":"The fixture awaits actual IPC admissions and listener registration instead of fabricating fallback IDs. An enabled version-cache button alone does not establish that React has committed its initial target-lifetime effect.","startLine":79,"endLine":79,"text":"The fixture awaits actual IPC admissions and listener registration instead of fabricating fallback IDs. An enabled version-cache button alone does not establish that React has committed its initial target-lifetime effect."},{"markdown":"Deferred metadata from the current initial load preserves a cached-ready update and its listener. A later connection-config notification still revokes the old phase and result by starting a new config epoch.","startLine":83,"endLine":83,"text":"Deferred metadata from the current initial load preserves a cached-ready update and its listener. A later connection-config notification still revokes the old phase and result by starting a new config epoch."},{"markdown":"The actual update handler echoes a caller's bounded request ID on command and validation events; legacy callers remain supported without an ID.","startLine":87,"endLine":87,"text":"The actual update handler echoes a caller's bounded request ID on command and validation events; legacy callers remain supported without an ID."},{"markdown":"Actual Install fixtures keep both one-step updates and final installer phases indeterminate while pending, and present 100 percent only after successful completion.","startLine":91,"endLine":91,"text":"Actual Install fixtures keep both one-step updates and final installer phases indeterminate while pending, and present 100 percent only after successful completion."},{"markdown":"The actual update IPC handler emits each SSH phase before its operation and keeps the pending command's total unknown without executing remote updates in tests.","startLine":95,"endLine":95,"text":"The actual update IPC handler emits each SSH phase before its operation and keeps the pending command's total unknown without executing remote updates in tests."},{"markdown":"The direct Remote mode keeps its existing host-side update guidance and never starts a local or SSH update through the desktop.","startLine":99,"endLine":99,"text":"The direct Remote mode keeps its existing host-side update guidance and never starts a local or SSH update through the desktop."},{"markdown":"Closing the window during each awaited SSH update stage skips later UI delivery while compatibility, gateway, tunnel and credential work completes normally.","startLine":103,"endLine":103,"text":"Closing the window during each awaited SSH update stage skips later UI delivery while compatibility, gateway, tunnel and credential work completes normally."},{"markdown":"A sender that disappears between its live check and send cannot abort update follow-up. Only progress delivery exceptions are discarded.","startLine":107,"endLine":107,"text":"A sender that disappears between its live check and send cannot abort update follow-up. Only progress delivery exceptions are discarded."},{"markdown":"Local command output remains safe after its window closes, and the update still validates compatibility and clears capability evidence.","startLine":111,"endLine":111,"text":"Local command output remains safe after its window closes, and the update still validates compatibility and clears capability evidence."},{"markdown":"The actual update callback still reports a failing SSH operation when no progress receiver remains; it neither hides the error nor runs unsafe later stages.","startLine":115,"endLine":115,"text":"The actual update callback still reports a failing SSH operation when no progress receiver remains; it neither hides the error nor runs unsafe later stages."},{"markdown":"The actual local updater receives synthetic child output and exit events; running and failed commands never turn their phase into measured completion.","startLine":119,"endLine":119,"text":"The actual local updater receives synthetic child output and exit events; running and failed commands never turn their phase into measured completion."},{"markdown":"Two GitHub Actions workflows publish builds; only the stable channel reaches end users' auto-update, so a beta can be tested without risking their devices.","startLine":123,"endLine":123,"text":"Two GitHub Actions workflows publish builds; only the stable channel reaches end users' auto-update, so a beta can be tested without risking their devices."},{"markdown":"`release.yml` (stable) runs on a push to the `release` branch: it tags `v<version>` from `package.json`, builds all platforms, and publishes a normal GitHub Release carrying the `latest*.yml` update feed. `beta-release.yml` runs on a push to `beta` (or manual dispatch): it stamps a prerelease version `v<version>-beta.<run>` via `scripts/set-version.mjs`, builds the same signed/notarized artifacts, and publishes a **GitHub prerelease** carrying a `beta*.yml` feed.","startLine":125,"endLine":125,"text":"release.yml (stable) runs on a push to the release branch: it tags v<version> from package.json, builds all platforms, and publishes a normal GitHub Release carrying the latest*.yml update feed. beta-release.yml runs on a push to beta (or manual dispatch): it stamps a prerelease version v<version>-beta.<run> via scripts/set-version.mjs, builds the same signed/notarized artifacts, and publishes a GitHub prerelease carrying a beta*.yml feed."},{"markdown":"Linux AppImage names include `${arch}` between version and extension, so x64 and arm64 outputs can coexist without overwriting one another. Stable and beta arm64 jobs also publish their architecture-specific updater feed (`latest-linux-arm64.yml` or `beta-linux-arm64.yml`) beside the installer. [[tests/release-artifacts.test.ts]] locks both invariants.","startLine":127,"endLine":127,"text":"Linux AppImage names include ${arch} between version and extension, so x64 and arm64 outputs can coexist without overwriting one another. Stable and beta arm64 jobs also publish their architecture-specific updater feed (latest-linux-arm64.yml or beta-linux-arm64.yml) beside the installer. tests/release-artifacts.test.ts locks both invariants."},{"markdown":"The isolation is structural: the updater ([[src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater]]) leaves `allowPrerelease` off, so electron-updater's GitHub provider only ever resolves the latest **non-prerelease** release's `latest.yml`. A beta prerelease is therefore invisible to stable clients — testers download the beta installer manually from the prerelease. The beta workflow skips winget + the landing-page rebuild and uses a separate `beta-release` concurrency group so it never cancels a stable release. Cutting a beta for the _next_ version requires bumping `package.json` first (a beta of an already-released version sorts lower than its stable tag).","startLine":129,"endLine":129,"text":"The isolation is structural: the updater (src/main/app/updater.ts#setupUpdater) leaves allowPrerelease off, so electron-updater's GitHub provider only ever resolves the latest non-prerelease release's latest.yml. A beta prerelease is therefore invisible to stable clients — testers download the beta installer manually from the prerelease. The beta workflow skips winget + the landing-page rebuild and uses a separate beta-release concurrency group so it never cancels a stable release. Cutting a beta for the next version requires bumping package.json first (a beta of an already-released version sorts lower than its stable tag)."},{"markdown":"Both release channels must attach the arm64 Linux updater feed to the GitHub release, in addition to preserving it between build jobs.","startLine":133,"endLine":133,"text":"Both release channels must attach the arm64 Linux updater feed to the GitHub release, in addition to preserving it between build jobs."},{"markdown":"The artifact regression parses the actual publish action's file list and checks its architecture-specific feed, preventing a successful CI upload from masking a missing public updater file. AppImage filenames remain distinct across architectures.","startLine":135,"endLine":135,"text":"The artifact regression parses the actual publish action's file list and checks its architecture-specific feed, preventing a successful CI upload from masking a missing public updater file. AppImage filenames remain distinct across architectures."},{"markdown":"Pull requests and main-branch pushes must have no lint warnings and no high-severity production dependency advisories before release work can proceed.","startLine":139,"endLine":139,"text":"Pull requests and main-branch pushes must have no lint warnings and no high-severity production dependency advisories before release work can proceed."},{"markdown":"The CI workflow installs the locked dependency tree, runs `npm run audit:prod`, type-checks, tests, and treats lint as blocking. The audit wrapper retries only transient registry/network failures; a reported high-severity vulnerability still fails immediately. The install step fetches the pinned Electron runtime once before tests, and the suite uses four workers so parallel imports never race a lazy binary download or starve short tests. [[tests/audit-production-dependencies.test.ts]] covers retry classification, while [[tests/release-artifacts.test.ts]] protects these controls, the audit threshold, and the zero-warning lint policy from accidental removal.","startLine":141,"endLine":141,"text":"The CI workflow installs the locked dependency tree, runs npm run audit:prod, type-checks, tests, and treats lint as blocking. The audit wrapper retries only transient registry/network failures; a reported high-severity vulnerability still fails immediately. The install step fetches the pinned Electron runtime once before tests, and the suite uses four workers so parallel imports never race a lazy binary download or starve short tests. tests/audit-production-dependencies.test.ts covers retry classification, while tests/release-artifacts.test.ts protects these controls, the audit threshold, and the zero-warning lint policy from accidental removal."},{"markdown":"The v0.7.8 candidate keeps the existing Electron major, raises its security floor to 44.9.1, and scopes an exact `undici` 7.30.2 override to the unchanged `@electron/get` 5.1.0 downloader. [Package metadata](../package.json) and the [lockfile](../package-lock.json) keep this limited patch reproducible; unrelated dependencies and the independent Agent update channel are unchanged.","startLine":143,"endLine":143,"text":"The v0.7.8 candidate keeps the existing Electron major, raises its security floor to 44.9.1, and scopes an exact undici 7.30.2 override to the unchanged @electron/get 5.1.0 downloader. Package metadata and the lockfile keep this limited patch reproducible; unrelated dependencies and the independent Agent update channel are unchanged."},{"markdown":"The production audit retries temporary npm registry and network failures without weakening vulnerability enforcement.","startLine":147,"endLine":147,"text":"The production audit retries temporary npm registry and network failures without weakening vulnerability enforcement."},{"markdown":"HTTP 5xx responses, DNS failures, resets, timeouts, and unreachable-registry errors receive up to three attempts with short backoff. Audit findings are not classified as transient and fail on the first attempt. `scripts/audit-production-dependencies.mjs` implements the classification boundary.","startLine":149,"endLine":149,"text":"HTTP 5xx responses, DNS failures, resets, timeouts, and unreachable-registry errors receive up to three attempts with short backoff. Audit findings are not classified as transient and fail on the first attempt. scripts/audit-production-dependencies.mjs implements the classification boundary."},{"markdown":"A completed audit findings report fails immediately even if a warning, advisory title or URL resembles a transient network failure.","startLine":153,"endLine":153,"text":"A completed audit findings report fails immediately even if a warning, advisory title or URL resembles a transient network failure."},{"markdown":"HTTP status matching requires an explicit HTTP or npm error context; bare `5xx` numbers inside advisory identifiers cannot trigger retry. [[tests/audit-production-dependencies.test.ts]] covers mixed registry warnings and vulnerability findings so release enforcement stays authoritative.","startLine":155,"endLine":155,"text":"HTTP status matching requires an explicit HTTP or npm error context; bare 5xx numbers inside advisory identifiers cannot trigger retry. tests/audit-production-dependencies.test.ts covers mixed registry warnings and vulnerability findings so release enforcement stays authoritative."},{"markdown":"Stable and beta release jobs import the Developer ID certificate into a workflow-owned temporary keychain before packaging, making signing reliable across GitHub runner updates.","startLine":159,"endLine":159,"text":"Stable and beta release jobs import the Developer ID certificate into a workflow-owned temporary keychain before packaging, making signing reliable across GitHub runner updates."},{"markdown":"`scripts/import-macos-certificate.sh` creates a random keychain password, adds the keychain to the user search list, imports the `.p12` with `CSC_KEY_PASSWORD`, and grants `codesign` access with the distinct keychain password required by macOS 26.6. Electron Builder receives only `CSC_KEYCHAIN`, so it cannot recreate the password mix-up. Both architecture jobs run even if one fails, verify that a Developer ID Application identity exists before packaging, and delete the temporary keychain afterward. [[tests/macos-signing.test.ts]] exercises keychain discovery, password separation, and credential cleanup; [[tests/release-artifacts.test.ts]] prevents either release channel from bypassing the staging flow.","startLine":161,"endLine":161,"text":"scripts/import-macos-certificate.sh creates a random keychain password, adds the keychain to the user search list, imports the .p12 with CSC_KEY_PASSWORD, and grants codesign access with the distinct keychain password required by macOS 26.6. Electron Builder receives only CSC_KEYCHAIN, so it cannot recreate the password mix-up. Both architecture jobs run even if one fails, verify that a Developer ID Application identity exists before packaging, and delete the temporary keychain afterward. tests/macos-signing.test.ts exercises keychain discovery, password separation, and credential cleanup; tests/release-artifacts.test.ts prevents either release channel from bypassing the staging flow."},{"markdown":"Signing verification consumes the complete identity list before deciding success, so early pipe closure cannot reject a valid Developer ID identity under `pipefail`.","startLine":165,"endLine":165,"text":"Signing verification consumes the complete identity list before deciding success, so early pipe closure cannot reject a valid Developer ID identity under pipefail."},{"markdown":"`scripts/import-macos-certificate.sh` retains failure propagation from the identity command and requires a matching identity. [[tests/macos-signing.test.ts]] exercises short output and a list exceeding pipe capacity, alongside password separation and credential cleanup.","startLine":167,"endLine":167,"text":"scripts/import-macos-certificate.sh retains failure propagation from the identity command and requires a matching identity. tests/macos-signing.test.ts exercises short output and a list exceeding pipe capacity, alongside password separation and credential cleanup."},{"markdown":"Stable and beta macOS builds verify the exact architecture-specific `better-sqlite3` prebuild that the packaged runtime loads.","startLine":171,"endLine":171,"text":"Stable and beta macOS builds verify the exact architecture-specific better-sqlite3 prebuild that the packaged runtime loads."},{"markdown":"Electron Builder explicitly unpacks `node_modules/better-sqlite3/prebuilds/*.node` from ASAR. After signing and notarization, `scripts/verify-native-module-architecture.sh` locates `darwin-x64.node` or `darwin-arm64.node` inside the packaged app and rejects a missing or mismatched binary. [[tests/release-artifacts.test.ts]] keeps the package rule and both release workflows aligned with the dependency's runtime layout.","startLine":173,"endLine":173,"text":"Electron Builder explicitly unpacks node_modules/better-sqlite3/prebuilds/*.node from ASAR. After signing and notarization, scripts/verify-native-module-architecture.sh locates darwin-x64.node or darwin-arm64.node inside the packaged app and rejects a missing or mismatched binary. tests/release-artifacts.test.ts keeps the package rule and both release workflows aligned with the dependency's runtime layout."},{"markdown":"The application archive contains compiled outputs, runtime resources, package metadata, the project license and production dependencies; repository development material is excluded.","startLine":177,"endLine":177,"text":"The application archive contains compiled outputs, runtime resources, package metadata, the project license and production dependencies; repository development material is excluded."},{"markdown":"The explicit file allowlist prevents local agent settings, test fixtures, alternate applications and mutable documentation from entering releases. Electron Builder still collects production dependencies separately, including unpacked native prebuilds. Nested environment files and npm configuration stay excluded even inside an allowed directory. [[tests/packaging-identity.test.ts]] exercises the installed builder's real application and dependency matchers.","startLine":179,"endLine":179,"text":"The explicit file allowlist prevents local agent settings, test fixtures, alternate applications and mutable documentation from entering releases. Electron Builder still collects production dependencies separately, including unpacked native prebuilds. Nested environment files and npm configuration stay excluded even inside an allowed directory. tests/packaging-identity.test.ts exercises the installed builder's real application and dependency matchers."},{"markdown":"Archive inputs must remain unchanged while packaging: a file whose length changes between header generation and streaming can invalidate offsets for every following entry. A completed package requires archive integrity and entrypoint checks before launch acceptance.","startLine":181,"endLine":181,"text":"Archive inputs must remain unchanged while packaging: a file whose length changes between header generation and streaming can invalidate offsets for every following entry. A completed package requires archive integrity and entrypoint checks before launch acceptance."},{"markdown":"Linux packages use the space-free `/opt/HermesOne` directory while macOS keeps the existing `Hermes One.app` bundle and executable name. RPM filenames retain the `.rpm` extension expected by release uploads.","startLine":185,"endLine":185,"text":"Linux packages use the space-free /opt/HermesOne directory while macOS keeps the existing Hermes One.app bundle and executable name. RPM filenames retain the .rpm extension expected by release uploads."},{"markdown":"The global packaging product name supplies Electron Builder's Linux install-directory component. The explicit macOS `executableName` preserves its bundle path, and platform display labels retain Hermes One. Linux package setup uses the builder's default hooks. [[tests/packaging-identity.test.ts]] validates the configuration with the installed Electron Builder schema and evaluates its real application metadata and artifact macros.","startLine":187,"endLine":187,"text":"The global packaging product name supplies Electron Builder's Linux install-directory component. The explicit macOS executableName preserves its bundle path, and platform display labels retain Hermes One. Linux package setup uses the builder's default hooks. tests/packaging-identity.test.ts validates the configuration with the installed Electron Builder schema and evaluates its real application metadata and artifact macros."},{"markdown":"DEB and RPM packages use Electron Builder's complete default post-install hook so compatible AppArmor policy is installed alongside sandbox permissions and desktop integration.","startLine":191,"endLine":191,"text":"DEB and RPM packages use Electron Builder's complete default post-install hook so compatible AppArmor policy is installed alongside sandbox permissions and desktop integration."},{"markdown":"The builder supplies `resources/apparmor-profile`, validates it against the installed parser, copies compatible policy into `/etc/apparmor.d/hermes-desktop`, and reloads it outside chroots. Disabled AppArmor and unsupported older parsers skip policy installation. Working user namespaces keep `chrome-sandbox` at 0755; other hosts use its SUID mode. The previous custom hook replaced this setup with an unconditional permission change and omitted policy installation.","startLine":193,"endLine":193,"text":"The builder supplies resources/apparmor-profile, validates it against the installed parser, copies compatible policy into /etc/apparmor.d/hermes-desktop, and reloads it outside chroots. Disabled AppArmor and unsupported older parsers skip policy installation. Working user namespaces keep chrome-sandbox at 0755; other hosts use its SUID mode. The previous custom hook replaced this setup with an unconditional permission change and omitted policy installation."},{"markdown":"[[tests/linux-post-install.test.ts]] generates the actual DEB/RPM scripts through the installed builder and executes them with temporary paths and harmless command fixtures. The POSIX execution suite skips Windows, where `/bin/bash` and shell fixtures are unavailable; cross-platform packaging/schema assertions remain active. No system policy, package installation, or native Ubuntu launch is exercised; packaged Ubuntu 24.04 acceptance remains necessary.","startLine":195,"endLine":195,"text":"tests/linux-post-install.test.ts generates the actual DEB/RPM scripts through the installed builder and executes them with temporary paths and harmless command fixtures. The POSIX execution suite skips Windows, where /bin/bash and shell fixtures are unavailable; cross-platform packaging/schema assertions remain active. No system policy, package installation, or native Ubuntu launch is exercised; packaged Ubuntu 24.04 acceptance remains necessary."},{"markdown":"Each native fixture subprocess has a 15-second hard deadline and its test has a 20-second ceiling. This accommodates measured shell-helper startup under parallel macOS test load while retaining exact output, policy and exit-code assertions; product timeouts are unchanged.","startLine":197,"endLine":197,"text":"Each native fixture subprocess has a 15-second hard deadline and its test has a 20-second ceiling. This accommodates measured shell-helper startup under parallel macOS test load while retaining exact output, policy and exit-code assertions; product timeouts are unchanged."},{"markdown":"Both actual generated package hooks copy the exact bundled profile bytes and load the installed policy after parser compatibility succeeds.","startLine":201,"endLine":201,"text":"Both actual generated package hooks copy the exact bundled profile bytes and load the installed policy after parser compatibility succeeds."},{"markdown":"Disabled AppArmor and an incompatible parser skip policy copying and kernel loading without turning a platform compatibility skip into a package failure.","startLine":205,"endLine":205,"text":"Disabled AppArmor and an incompatible parser skip policy copying and kernel loading without turning a platform compatibility skip into a package failure."},{"markdown":"A compatible profile is copied into an image-building chroot while kernel loading is skipped, preserving the host's policy state.","startLine":209,"endLine":209,"text":"A compatible profile is copied into an image-building chroot while kernel loading is skipped, preserving the host's policy state."},{"markdown":"A compatible policy that fails actual kernel loading retains its nonzero result, rather than being silently treated as an unsupported parser.","startLine":213,"endLine":213,"text":"A compatible policy that fails actual kernel loading retains its nonzero result, rather than being silently treated as an unsupported parser."},{"markdown":"Working user namespaces preserve sandbox mode 0755, while unavailable namespaces select the SUID mode needed by Chromium's alternate sandbox path.","startLine":217,"endLine":217,"text":"Working user namespaces preserve sandbox mode 0755, while unavailable namespaces select the SUID mode needed by Chromium's alternate sandbox path."}],"blocks":[{"type":"heading","start":0,"end":17,"startLine":1,"endLine":1,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":19,"end":135,"startLine":3,"endLine":3,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":137,"end":499,"startLine":5,"endLine":5,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":501,"end":812,"startLine":7,"endLine":7,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":814,"end":1669,"startLine":9,"endLine":9,"children":[]},{"type":"heading","start":1671,"end":1702,"startLine":11,"endLine":11,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":1704,"end":1876,"startLine":13,"endLine":13,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":1878,"end":2407,"startLine":15,"endLine":15,"children":[]},{"type":"heading","start":2409,"end":2429,"startLine":17,"endLine":17,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":2431,"end":2613,"startLine":19,"endLine":19,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":2615,"end":3036,"startLine":21,"endLine":21,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":3038,"end":3349,"startLine":23,"endLine":23,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":3351,"end":3572,"startLine":25,"endLine":25,"children":[]},{"type":"heading","start":3574,"end":3602,"startLine":27,"endLine":27,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":3604,"end":3765,"startLine":29,"endLine":29,"children":[]},{"type":"heading","start":3767,"end":3809,"startLine":31,"endLine":31,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","start":3811,"end":3960,"startLine":33,"endLine":33,"children":[]},{"type":"heading","start":3962,"end":3997,"startLine":35,"endLine":35,"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","sta